WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.080
Good morning, Hank.
It's Tuesday.

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:04.180
So, over at YouTube.com/100days,

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:06.000
I'm in the midsts of
a health and fitness journey

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.200
with my best friend, Chris.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.760
Hold up -- did I just say
I'm on a journey?

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.500
Hank, I'm becoming a self-help book.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.860
Speaking of which, I have long
been an avowed enemy

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:14.300
of the motivational quote.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.400
You know, like
"Tough times don't last,

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:17.680
but tough people do."

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.500
Or "Nothing worth having
comes easy,"

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.140
or "What's the point of being alive
if you don't at least try

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:23.560
to do something remarkable?"

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.940
Which is actually a quote from my book,
𝘈𝘯 𝘈𝘣𝘶𝘯𝘥𝘢𝘯𝘤𝘦 𝘰𝘧 𝘒𝘢𝘵𝘩𝘦𝘳𝘪𝘯𝘦𝘴,

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:26.900
so my bad.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.860
My first problem with motivational quotes
is that, like, they aren't true.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:31.760
Like, tough people don't last.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.560
Like all people, they die.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:35.920
And many things that are
worth having come easily.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:39.220
Like bananas, for instance, are
available at almost any supermarket

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:40.400
for like 50 cents.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.960
Also, hopefully, the characters
in 𝘈𝘯 𝘈𝘣𝘶𝘯𝘥𝘢𝘯𝘤𝘦 𝘰𝘧 𝘒𝘢𝘵𝘩𝘦𝘳𝘪𝘯𝘦𝘴

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.600
spend that novel learning
that an unremarkable life

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.060
can be fulfilling and meaningful.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.840
Then there's that old Walt Disney line
"If you can dream it, you can do it,"

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.520
which has appeared on more posters
than Justin Beiber's face

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:54.440
despite being manifestly untrue.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:56.060
Like, have you ever
even had a dream?

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:57.960
I once had a dream I was a Tuatara.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.820
And if the mountain we must conquer
exists within ourselves,

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:03.660
then why did you climb all those
actual, physical mountains,

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:04.800
sir Edmund Hillary?

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.260
You should have been
at home, meditating,

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:07.840
conquering the mountain
within yourself.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.820
Also, just for the record, neither
Walt Disney nor Sir Edmund Hillary

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.520
ever said those quotes that are
so often attributed to them.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.740
The motivational quote plague
is perhaps most notable

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:17.020
at workout facilities,

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.940
like the gym where Chris and I
usually work out has

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:21.800
this Steve Prefontaine quote
about how giving less than your best

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.620
is sacrificing your gift.

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:26.360
And a boxing gym we visited had
a hand-written sign reading --

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.900
You don't have to get ready
if you stay ready.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:30.900
I find these little aphorisms,
not just annoying,

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.560
but kind of insulting. . .

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:33.540
and yet. . .

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:36.000
Not to brag or anything, but
I ran 9 miles yesterday,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.320
and around mile 4,
I found myself thinking --

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:40.480
you don't have to get ready,
if you stay ready.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:41.960
God help me, Hank.
I've become a fan

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.080
of motivational quotes.
I'm on a frickin' journey.

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.060
I kind of feel like my whole
adult life has been

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:48.740
a series of circumstances
that force me to reckon seriously

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:50.740
with stuff I once easily dismissed.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:53.180
Like I remember living in New York in 2007

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:56.380
and railing against the sheeple
buying houses in the Midwest.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:59.380
And now I'm a homeowner in Indianapolis
and I quite like it.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:02.180
I used to think that bestselling
books were, by definition, crap,

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:04.060
and then I found myself quickly backtracking

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:06.220
when I unexpectedly
became the author of one.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:09.600
I've ridiculed parents for focusing
on their kids instead of the world's kids,

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.040
only to become a parent and
understand that impulse,

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.420
and I've said that popular music was dead

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:17.120
only to really like Beyonce's album, "Lemonade."

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.260
But even now I still haven't learned
that when I want to roll my eyes,

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:22.420
I should probably be alive
to the possibility

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:26.260
that the object of my disdain is
more complex than I'm imagining.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:29.420
And so, now yes I've become a person
who revels in clichéd,

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:33.760
usually miss-attributed quotes in order to
motivate me toward physical fitness.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:35.260
This is a surprise on several levels,

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:37.940
not least because I have long
dismissed physical fitness

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:41.500
as a waste of energy, when all that
really matters about humans is their minds.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:43.280
But these past few weeks have already taught me

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.380
that there is no hard line between
the body and the brain,

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:48.540
that running can be good for my writing,

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:50.120
and writing good for my running.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:53.360
I kind of thought I would have to
abandon like my real work for 100 days

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.140
while I focused on my physical health,

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:58.860
but, instead, I'm finding myself enjoying writing
more than I have in years.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.620
And even though I'm spending
more time working out,

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:03.660
weirdly, I find that I have
more time for writing, too.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.700
These days, I'm at the keyboard
pretty much every day.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:08.060
Because, you know,
you don't have to get ready,

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:09.260
if you stay ready.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:11.040
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:12.280
End screen.

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:15.340
Four things. I will be at VidCon Europe
in Amsterdam in April

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:17.400
with many other YouTubers.
Hope to see you there.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.140
Check out 100 days.
I also wanted to let you know

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.100
that these 10th anniversary
VlogBrothers woodcuts

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.760
are available for a couple
more weeks at DFTBA.com,

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.960
and, lastly, Rosianna's self-care bunny
wants you to know. . .

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:28.160
that you are enough.

